==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|Teutonick}}

===Etymology===
1580, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|Teutōnicus}}, from {{m|la|Teutōnes}}, {{m|la|Teutōnī}} {{gloss|"the Teutons"; name of a Germanic tribe that inhabited coastal Germany and devastated Gaul between 113-101 B.C.}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|Teuton|ic}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˌtjuːˈtɒn.ɪk/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɒnɪk|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}
{{wikipedia}}

# Relating to the ancient [[Germanic]] people, the [[Teutons]].
# Having qualities that are regarded as typical of [[German]] people.
#: '''''Teutonic''' exactitude''
#* '''1886''', {{w|Henry James}}, ''The Princess Casamassima''
#*: He waited and waited, in the faith that Schinkel was dealing with them in his slow, categorical '''Teutonic''' way, and only objurgated the cabinetmaker for having in the first place paltered with his sacred trust. Why hadn't he come straight to him—whatever the mysterious document was—instead of talking it over with French featherheads?
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[Germanic]] branch of Indo-European family of languages

